--TEST--
`Core\Arr::padStart` and `::padEnd` count the shortfall from the subject's own count, place one and the same run on opposite sides, and repeat the one `$value` rather than a copy of it — spec § 2, `rule:types/preserve-keys`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members share `padding` and `append_copies`, so what is worth
// asserting is not a row of either one's output —
// `arr-pad-members-always-return-a-list.nvst` has those, including the keys
// this one deliberately does not re-ask about — but the three facts that hold
// across every subject: the shortfall is a function of the subject's *count*
// alone, at or below that count both members are the identity on the values,
// and whatever run one places in front the other places behind.
//
// Written as an agreement over a sweep, which is what catches a member that
// grew its own answer: a `padStart` that counted a map's keys, or that stopped
// one entry short, still reads plausibly beside its own arguments.
final class Pad {
    /// Whether both members reach the wider of count and `$size`, mirror each
    /// other, and leave the values alone where nothing is owed.
    public static function agrees(array<string> $subject, uint $size, string $value): int {
        uint $have = Core\Arr::count($subject);
        uint $want = $size;
        if ($have > $want) {
            $want = $have;
        }
        array<string> $before = Core\Arr::padStart($subject, $size, $value);
        array<string> $after = Core\Arr::padEnd($subject, $size, $value);
        // The shortfall is `$size` less the subject's own count, saturating —
        // so a map's keys never enter it, and a `$size` below the count owes
        // no entries rather than a negative number of them.
        if (Core\Arr::count($before) != $want) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (Core\Arr::count($after) != $want) {
            return 0;
        }
        // One run, two sides: reversing what `padStart` built is exactly what
        // `padEnd` builds over the reversed subject. A member that padded to a
        // different width, or that placed its run on the side the other names,
        // fails here while both of its own lines still look right.
        array<string> $mirror = Core\Arr::padEnd(Core\Arr::reverse($subject), $size, $value);
        if (Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::reverse($before), ",") != Core\Str::join($mirror, ",")) {
            return 0;
        }
        // At or below the count the answer is the identity **on the values**;
        // it is not the identity on the keys, which the sibling case pins.
        if ($have >= $size) {
            string $values = Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::values($subject), ",");
            if (Core\Str::join($before, ",") != $values) {
                return 0;
            }
            if (Core\Str::join($after, ",") != $values) {
                return 0;
            }
        }
        return 1;
    }
}

// Five subjects: empty, a one-entry list, a two-entry list, a map whose keys
// are names, and a map whose keys are digit strings in neither insertion nor
// numeric order — the last is the one that would catch a shortfall counted
// from a key rather than from the count.
array<array<string>> $subjects = [
    [],
    ["a"],
    ["one", "two"],
    ["x" => "one", "y" => "two"],
    ["9" => "one", "0" => "two", "k" => "three"],
];

int $agreed = 0;
int $asked = 0;
uint $one = 1;
uint $size = 0;
uint $sizes = 5;
foreach ($subjects as array<string> $subject) {
    $size = 0;
    while ($size < $sizes) {
        $asked = $asked + 1;
        $agreed = $agreed + Pad::agrees($subject, $size, "-");
        $size = $size + $one;
    }
}
echo "both count the shortfall and mirror each other: ", $agreed, " of ", $asked, "\n";

// The same fact said once without the sweep: a map and a list of the same
// count are owed the same number of entries, so the two results differ only in
// the values the subject brought.
array<string> $map = ["x" => "one", "y" => "two"];
array<string> $list = ["one", "two"];
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::padStart($map, 4, "-"), ","), "\n";
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::padStart($list, 4, "-"), ","), "\n";

// And the padded entries are the one `$value`, not a copy of it: `append_copies`
// stores a fresh reference to the same value each time, so where that value is
// an object every added entry is that object. Mutating it through the original
// handle is visible in all three.
final class Box {
    public int $n = 0;
}
Box $b = new Box();
array<Box> $none = [];
array<Box> $boxes = Core\Arr::padEnd($none, 3, $b);
$b->n = 7;
echo Core\Arr::count($boxes), " ", $boxes["0"]->n, $boxes["1"]->n, $boxes["2"]->n, "\n";
// Identity, not just equal contents — `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality` makes `==` on two objects
// exactly the identity comparison this needs.
if ($boxes["0"] == $boxes["2"]) {
    echo "one value, repeated\n";
} else {
    echo "copied\n";
}
--EXPECT--
both count the shortfall and mirror each other: 25 of 25
-,-,one,two
-,-,one,two
3 777
one value, repeated
